        ],
        'content'   => '<p class="lead">If you are weighing a <strong>Trump account vs 529</strong> plan, the short version is that they are built for different jobs. A 529 plan is an education savings account with tax-free withdrawals for qualified school costs. A Trump account is a new type of traditional IRA for children, created by Public Law 119-21, that is locked until the year the child turns 18 and then follows most IRA rules. This guide compares both with a custodial UTMA/UGMA account and a Roth IRA for kids, then runs the numbers. For the basics of the program, start with our <a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained</a> hub.</p>'
            . '<p><em>Trump Account rules are set by IRS Notice 2025-68, the Form 4547 instructions and temporary and proposed regulations. Final regulations have not been issued, so check IRS.gov before acting.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: where Trump accounts stand in October 2026</h2><p>Several developments since the program launched affect the Trump account vs 529 comparison:</p><ul><li><strong>July 4, 2026:</strong> contributions opened and Treasury began depositing the $1,000 pilot contributions, according to Treasury’s May 28 and July 4, 2026 releases. Treasury has not published a count of deposits made so far in the releases we reviewed.</li><li><strong>October 1, 2026:</strong> Treasury completed automatic enrollment, saying over 60 million more eligible children now have an account ready to be claimed. A parent must still claim the account, and the $1,000 still requires a Form 4547 election.</li><li><strong>Regulations:</strong> proposed rules came on March 6, 2026 (IR-2026-33), and temporary regulations (T.D. 10056, effective September 30, 2026) cover auto-enrolled accounts. Final regulations have not been issued.</li><li><strong>Portal:</strong> the Trump Accounts app launched May 28, 2026, and Form 4547 can also be filed in the IRS Individual Online Account (IR-2026-68).</li><li><strong>Dell gift:</strong> Invest America says all $250 Dell deposits were made as of October 9, 2026.</li><li><strong>Employers:</strong> Treasury issued employer guidance on August 11, 2026 and said more than 50 companies had committed to contributions.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What is the difference between a Trump account and a 529?</h2><p>The difference between a Trump account and a 529 comes down to purpose and taxes. A 529 plan is a state-sponsored education account: money grows tax-deferred and comes out federally tax-free when it pays for qualified education expenses. A Trump account is an individual retirement account for a child. According to the IRS Form 4547 instructions, no tax deduction is allowed for contributions, money generally cannot be withdrawn during the “growth period,” and starting January 1 of the year the child turns 18, most traditional IRA rules apply. That means earnings are taxable when withdrawn, and an early-withdrawal penalty can apply unless an exception such as higher education or a first home purchase fits.</p>'
            . '<p>In practice, a 529 vs Trump account decision is rarely either-or. A 529 is the stronger tool for college. A Trump account is a long-horizon account that can collect money from sources a 529 cannot easily tap, such as the $1,000 federal pilot deposit, tax-free employer contributions and philanthropic deposits.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account vs 529 vs UTMA vs Roth IRA: comparison table</h2>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Trump account</th><th>529 plan</th><th>Custodial UTMA/UGMA</th><th>Roth IRA for kids</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Yearly contribution limit</td><td>$5,000 total from family, friends and employers (2026 and 2027, indexed after 2027); the $1,000 pilot and qualified general contributions are outside the limit</td><td>No federal yearly limit; each plan sets a lifetime cap; gifts above $19,000 per giver per child in 2026 may need a gift tax return</td><td>No limit; gift tax rules apply above $19,000 per giver in 2026</td><td>The child’s earned income or $7,500 (2026), whichever is less</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Federal tax on contributions</td><td>Not deductible; employer contributions up to $2,500 excluded from the employee’s income</td><td>Not federally deductible; many states offer a deduction or credit</td><td>Not deductible</td><td>Not deductible</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Tax on growth</td><td>Tax-deferred</td><td>Tax-deferred</td><td>Taxed yearly; for 2026, a child’s unearned income above $2,700 can be taxed at the parent’s rate (kiddie tax)</td><td>Tax-free if rules are met</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Tax on withdrawals</td><td>Earnings and no-basis money (pilot, employer, general contributions) taxed as ordinary income; family contributions come back tax-free pro rata</td><td>Tax-free for qualified education; earnings taxed plus 10% penalty otherwise (exceptions apply)</td><td>No extra tax at withdrawal beyond yearly taxes</td><td>Contributions any time tax-free; earnings tax-free after age 59½ and five years</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Who can contribute</td><td>Parents, relatives, anyone; employers; governments and charities through general contributions</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Anyone, but only up to the child’s earned income</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Withdrawal rules</td><td>Generally none before January 1 of the year the child turns 18; IRA rules after</td><td>Any time; tax depends on use</td><td>Custodian may spend for the child’s benefit; child takes control at the state’s age of majority (often 18 or 21)</td><td>Contributions any time; earnings restricted</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Investment options</td><td>Only mutual funds or ETFs tracking a mostly U.S. stock index, no leverage, fees of 0.1% or less</td><td>Plan menu of portfolios</td><td>Almost anything</td><td>Almost anything</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Financial aid (FAFSA)</td><td>Not yet addressed in Federal Student Aid guidance</td><td>Parent-owned 529 reported as a parent asset for a dependent student</td><td>Reported as the student’s asset</td><td>Retirement accounts are not reported as assets</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Government seed money</td><td>$1,000 for U.S. citizen children born 2025 to 2028, if elected</td><td>None federally; some states offer seed programs</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Sources for the table: IRS Notice 2025-68 and the Form 4547 instructions (Trump account rules), IR-2025-111 (2026 IRA limit), Rev. Proc. 2025-32 (2026 kiddie tax amount) and the IRS gift tax FAQ (2026 annual exclusion). The financial aid row reflects studentaid.gov guidance on education savings and custodial accounts; we could not find any Federal Student Aid guidance on Trump accounts, so treat that cell as unconfirmed.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account for kids vs 529: which grows more?</h2><p>Both accounts can hold stock index funds, so before taxes the growth is similar for the same money. The Trump account for kids vs 529 comparison changes on two points: the extra $1,000 that an eligible child can receive, and the tax when the money comes out.</p>'
            . '<h3>Worked example: $2,000 a year for 18 years</h3><p>Assume a child born in 2026, a 7% average yearly return, and a family that adds $2,000 at the end of each year for 18 years. These are illustrations, not forecasts; real returns vary and can be negative.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Scenario (7% a year, 18 years)</th><th>Trump account</th><th>529 plan</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Family contributions</td><td>$36,000</td><td>$36,000</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Federal pilot deposit</td><td>$1,000</td><td>$0</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Balance at about age 18</td><td>$71,378</td><td>$67,998</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Same, at 4% a year</td><td>$53,317</td><td>$51,291</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Same, at 10% a year</td><td>$96,758</td><td>$91,198</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Federally taxable if spent on college</td><td>About $35,378 (earnings plus the $1,000, which has no basis), with the 10% penalty generally waived for higher education</td><td>$0 for qualified education expenses</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>The $1,000 deposit alone grows to about $3,380 over 18 years at 7%, which is why the Trump account starts ahead. But if the money pays for college, the 529 can win after tax. In the Trump account, the $36,000 of family contributions creates basis and comes back tax-free, while the remaining $35,378 is ordinary income in the year it is withdrawn. Even at a 10% federal bracket, that is about $3,538 of tax, which more than cancels the $3,380 head start. If the money is left invested for retirement instead, the comparison shifts again, because the Trump account becomes a regular IRA-style account that can keep growing for decades. Run your own numbers in the <a href="{tool:trump-account-calculator}">Trump account calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:compound-interest-calculator}">compound interest calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account vs UTMA: what about a custodial account?</h2><p>The Trump account vs UTMA question (also searched as trump account vs custodial account) is about flexibility against tax breaks. A UTMA or UGMA custodial account belongs to the child from day one. The custodian, usually a parent, can spend it for the child’s benefit and can invest in almost anything. The trade-off is taxes: investment income is taxed every year, and for 2026 a child’s unearned income above $2,700 can be taxed at the parent’s rate under the kiddie tax. At the state’s age of majority, often 18 or 21, the child takes full control and can use the money for anything.</p><p>A Trump account is narrower but tax-deferred. It must hold a low-cost U.S. stock index fund, and the money is generally locked until the year the child turns 18. After that, the account is still an IRA, so withdrawals are taxed and can carry a 10% early-withdrawal penalty unless an exception applies. A UTMA suits families who want money available for the child before 18; a Trump account suits money meant to stay invested.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account vs Roth IRA for kids</h2><p>The Trump account vs Roth IRA comparison looks close on paper because both are IRAs for a child, but the rules differ sharply. A Roth IRA for kids needs earned income: the child can contribute only up to what they earned that year, capped at $7,500 for 2026. A baby or toddler has no earned income, so a Roth IRA is not an option until the child has a job or legitimate self-employment income. A Trump account needs no earned income.</p><p>The tax treatment is also opposite. Roth contributions can be withdrawn at any time without tax, and earnings are tax-free after age 59½ once the account has been open five years. A Trump account becomes a traditional-style IRA after the growth period, so earnings are taxed when withdrawn. Once a teenager has a summer job, a Roth IRA is often the stronger retirement account. Our <a href="{tool:ira-calculator}">IRA calculator</a> can show the long-run difference.</p>'
            . '<h2>Can you have both a Trump account and a 529 plan?</h2><p>Yes. Nothing in the IRS guidance stops a child from having a Trump account, a 529 plan, a custodial account and, once they earn money, a Roth IRA. Each has its own limit. A common order of priorities is:</p><ol><li><strong>Claim the Trump account and elect the $1,000</strong> if your child was born 2025 to 2028, because the deposit is free money that needs no contribution from you.</li><li><strong>Fund a 529 plan for expected education costs</strong>, especially if your state offers a tax deduction.</li><li><strong>Use the Trump account for extra long-term money</strong>, particularly if an employer offers tax-free contributions of up to $2,500 a year.</li><li><strong>Open a Roth IRA once the child has earned income.</strong></li></ol>'
            . '<h2>529 plan vs Trump account: when each one wins</h2><h3>The 529 plan usually wins when</h3><ul><li>The money is for college, trade school or other qualified education costs.</li><li>Your state gives a deduction or credit for 529 contributions.</li><li>You want to keep control: the account owner, not the child, controls a 529.</li><li>You may want to roll leftover money into the beneficiary’s Roth IRA later, which the law allows within a lifetime limit and other conditions.</li></ul>'
            . '<h3>The Trump account usually wins when</h3><ul><li>Your child is eligible for the $1,000 pilot deposit or has received a philanthropic deposit.</li><li>An employer offers tax-free contributions to employees’ children’s accounts.</li><li>The goal is long-term wealth or retirement rather than school costs.</li><li>You want a simple, low-cost index fund with fees capped at 0.1% a year.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes when comparing the accounts</h2><ul><li><strong>Treating a Trump account as a college fund.</strong> It can pay for college after age 18, but the earnings are taxed. A 529 is designed for that job.</li><li><strong>Contributing more than $5,000 a year.</strong> The Trump account limit covers family and employer money combined.</li><li><strong>Assuming contributions are deductible.</strong> Family contributions to a Trump account are not deductible on your federal return.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the investment limits.</strong> A Trump account can hold only a U.S. stock index fund, so it is fully exposed to the stock market with no bond option during the growth period.</li><li><strong>Forgetting the claim step.</strong> Treasury automatically enrolled eligible children from October 1, 2026, but a parent must claim the account before family members can contribute. See <a href="/how-to-open-a-trump-account/">how to open a Trump account</a>.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>For the fund type the Trump account uses, read our <a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">index fund investing guide</a>. To set a target for college savings, try the <a href="{tool:savings-goal-calculator}">savings goal calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained: who qualifies, limits and how to open one</a></li><li><a href="/how-to-open-a-trump-account/">How to open a Trump account: Form 4547 step by step</a></li><li><a href="/trump-account-250-dell-employer/">Trump account $250: Dell gift and employer contributions</a></li><li><a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">Index fund investing for beginners</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-25-68.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Notice 2025-68</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4547" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Instructions for Form 4547</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/faqs/trump-accounts-frequently-asked-questions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Trump Accounts FAQ</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IR-2025-111 (2026 IRA limit)</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rev. Proc. 2025-32</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/frequently-asked-questions-on-gift-taxes" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS gift tax FAQ</a>; <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0642" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Treasury, October 1, 2026</a>. Growth figures are our own calculations at the stated returns.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'Is a Trump account better than a 529?', 'Not for college costs. A 529 plan allows federally tax-free withdrawals for qualified education expenses, while Trump account earnings are taxed when withdrawn. A Trump account can collect a $1,000 federal deposit and tax-free employer money, so it can be better for long-term savings. Many families use both accounts for different goals.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between a Trump account and a 529?', 'A 529 is an education savings plan with tax-free withdrawals for school costs and no federal yearly limit. A Trump account is a traditional IRA for a child, limited to $5,000 a year from family and employers, invested only in U.S. stock index funds and generally locked until January 1 of the year the child turns 18.' ],
            [ 'Trump account vs UTMA: which is better?', 'A UTMA custodial account is flexible: it can be spent for the child at any age and invested in almost anything, but income is taxed yearly. A Trump account is tax-deferred and low cost, but locked until the year the child turns 18 and taxed like an IRA afterward. The better choice depends on timing.' ],
            [ 'Trump account vs Roth IRA for kids: what is the difference?', 'A Roth IRA for kids needs earned income and allows tax-free growth, with contributions capped at the lesser of earnings or $7,500 for 2026. A Trump account needs no earned income but becomes a traditional-style IRA after the growth period, so its earnings are taxed when withdrawn. A working teenager often benefits from both.' ],
            [ 'Can a child have a Trump account and a 529 at the same time?', 'Yes. The IRS guidance does not prevent a child from having a Trump account alongside a 529 plan, a custodial account or a Roth IRA. Each account follows its own contribution limits and tax rules, so families can put college money in the 529 and long-term money in the Trump account.' ],
            [ 'Does a Trump account affect financial aid?', 'That is not yet confirmed. Federal Student Aid has not published guidance on how Trump accounts are treated on the FAFSA. Parent-owned 529 plans count as parent assets for dependent students, custodial accounts count as student assets, and retirement accounts are not reported. Check studentaid.gov for updates before you rely on any treatment.' ],
            [ 'Are Trump account contributions tax deductible like some 529 plans?', 'No. Family contributions to a Trump account are not deductible on the federal return, according to the IRS. 529 contributions are not federally deductible either, but many states offer a state income tax deduction or credit. Employer contributions to a Trump account of up to $2,500 a year are excluded from the employee’s income.' ],
        ],
