        ],
        'publish_at' => '2026-10-26 09:00',
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">This guide explains <strong>how to open a Trump account</strong> for your child in late 2026, when the process changed. On October 1, 2026, the Treasury Department automatically opened accounts for eligible children, so for most families opening a Trump account now means claiming the account that already exists and, for babies born 2025 to 2028, filing IRS Form 4547 to request the $1,000 federal deposit. Below are the steps, the documents you need and a decision checklist. For program basics, see our <a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained</a> hub.</p>'
            . '<p><em>The IRS has issued temporary and proposed regulations, not final ones. Procedures can change, so confirm each step on IRS.gov and trumpaccounts.gov.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: where Trump accounts stand in October 2026</h2><p>The sign-up process has changed twice this year. The newest verified developments:</p><ul><li><strong>July 4, 2026:</strong> contributions opened and Treasury began depositing the $1,000 pilot contributions, according to Treasury’s May 28 and July 4, 2026 releases. Treasury has not published a count of deposits made so far in the releases we reviewed.</li><li><strong>October 1, 2026:</strong> Treasury completed automatic enrollment, saying over 60 million more eligible children now have an account ready to be claimed. A parent must still claim the account, and the $1,000 still requires a Form 4547 election.</li><li><strong>Regulations:</strong> proposed rules came on March 6, 2026 (IR-2026-33), and temporary regulations (T.D. 10056, effective September 30, 2026) cover auto-enrolled accounts. Final regulations have not been issued.</li><li><strong>Portal:</strong> the Trump Accounts app launched May 28, 2026, and Form 4547 can also be filed in the IRS Individual Online Account (IR-2026-68).</li><li><strong>Dell gift:</strong> Invest America says all $250 Dell deposits were made as of October 9, 2026.</li><li><strong>Employers:</strong> Treasury issued employer guidance on August 11, 2026 and said more than 50 companies had committed to contributions.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to open a Trump account in 2026: the short version</h2><p>Here is how to open a Trump account or claim one, according to the IRS Trump Accounts FAQ and Treasury’s October 1, 2026 announcement:</p><ol><li><strong>Check whether your child already has an account.</strong> Treasury says every eligible child under 18 with a valid Social Security number now has one. The Trump Accounts app or website shows whether an account exists.</li><li><strong>Claim and activate it.</strong> Download the official Trump Accounts app from the Apple App Store or Google Play, or use the Trump Accounts website. Verify your identity and your relationship to the child, review the child’s details and accept the account terms.</li><li><strong>Elect the $1,000 if your child qualifies.</strong> File Form 4547 and check the pilot program box (line 7). Automatic enrollment does not include this election.</li><li><strong>Add contributions if you wish</strong>, up to $5,000 a year in total from family and employers.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>What is Form 4547?</h2><p>Form 4547, “Trump Account Election(s),” is the official IRS form used to elect to open an initial Trump account for a child and to elect the $1,000 pilot program contribution from Treasury. The IRS FAQ confirms it is a legitimate IRS form. The instructions (Rev. December 2025) say it can be filed at any time. Since automatic enrollment, its main use is the $1,000 election, and it remains the route to open an account for a child who was not auto-enrolled, for example because the Social Security number was issued after October 1, 2026.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to sign up for a Trump account: Form 4547 step by step</h2><h3>Step 1: Confirm who can sign</h3><p>The person filing is the “authorized individual.” If you are only opening an account, the Form 4547 instructions list a legal guardian, parent, adult sibling or grandparent, in that order of priority. If you are also electing the $1,000, the authorized individual is the person who expects the child to be their qualifying child for that tax year. On the 2025 return you did not need to have claimed the child as a dependent.</p>'
            . '<h3>Step 2: Check the child’s eligibility</h3><ul><li><strong>For an account:</strong> the child is under 18 at the end of the election year (for 2026, born after December 31, 2008), has a valid Social Security number issued before the election, and has no earlier Trump account election.</li><li><strong>For the $1,000 pilot deposit:</strong> the child also must be a U.S. citizen, born after December 31, 2024 and before January 1, 2029, with no earlier pilot election processed.</li></ul>'
            . '<h3>Step 3: Fill in the form</h3><ul><li><strong>Part I:</strong> your name, address and Social Security number (or ITIN if you are not eligible for an SSN).</li><li><strong>Part II:</strong> the child’s name, SSN, date of birth, relationship and address; check line 6 if you are authorized to open the account. Use extra copies for more than two children.</li><li><strong>Part III:</strong> check line 7 to request the $1,000 pilot contribution.</li><li><strong>Part IV:</strong> consent to disclosure, then sign and date. Paper forms need a handwritten signature.</li></ul>'
            . '<h3>Step 4: Choose where to file</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Filing method</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Trump Accounts app or website</td><td>The IRS FAQ says Form 4547 can be submitted through the app or the website; the IRS also points to an online form at form.trumpaccounts.gov</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>IRS Individual Online Account</td><td>Since May 28, 2026 (IR-2026-68), you can submit Form 4547 and track its status there</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>With an e-filed tax return</td><td>The instructions call this the fastest way when you are filing a return anyway</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>On paper</td><td>Mail to the address at IRS.gov/PaperReturns for your return, using the address for refunds or no payment enclosed</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table><p>Do not attach Form 4547 to Form 1040-X or amend a return to add it. If the IRS sends Notice CP318, follow that notice to resubmit, and make sure names, SSNs and birth dates match Social Security records.</p>'
            . '<h3>Step 5: Activate and manage the account</h3><p>After the election, Treasury or its agent sends instructions to activate the account, which requires identity verification. The person who activates it becomes the “responsible party” while the child is a minor, with the power to choose among eligible investments and name a successor. According to the IRS FAQ, BNY is Treasury’s financial agent and Robinhood Securities is the brokerage provider and initial trustee. The help line is 866-USA-4547 (866-872-4547).</p>'
            . '<h2>How to set up a Trump account for my child: documents needed</h2><ul><li>Your Social Security number (or ITIN) and a government ID for identity verification.</li><li>Your child’s Social Security card, with the name exactly as the SSA has it.</li><li>Your child’s date of birth and proof of U.S. citizenship status if you want the $1,000 (the form asks you to attest to it).</li><li>A smartphone for the app, or a computer for the website or IRS Online Account.</li><li>Bank account details if you plan to contribute.</li></ul><p>Most delays come from mismatches. The IRS says names, Social Security numbers and dates of birth must match Social Security Administration records.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account sign up timeline: key dates</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happened</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>July 4, 2025</td><td>Public Law 119-21 creates Trump accounts</td><td>IR-2026-68</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>December 2, 2025</td><td>Notice 2025-68 and Form 4547 released</td><td>IRS</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>March 6, 2026</td><td>Proposed regulations on opening accounts</td><td>IR-2026-33</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>May 28, 2026</td><td>Trump Accounts app launched; Form 4547 added to IRS Online Account</td><td>Treasury, IR-2026-68</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>July 4, 2026</td><td>Contributions open; first $1,000 pilot deposits</td><td>Treasury</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>October 1, 2026</td><td>Automatic enrollment of eligible children completed</td><td>Treasury, IRS FAQ</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>December 31 of the year the child turns 17</td><td>Last day to make an election under the proposed rules</td><td>IR-2026-33</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>How to open a Trump account for kids born before 2025</h2><p>For older children the steps are the same minus the $1,000. If your child was born in 2009 or later, is under 18 and has an SSN, Treasury’s auto-enrollment should already have created an account. Claim it in the app so relatives and employers can contribute. Some older children also received a $250 philanthropic deposit from Michael and Susan Dell; see our guide to the <a href="/trump-account-250-dell-employer/">Trump account $250 and employer contributions</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: what claiming early can mean</h2><p>Assume a 7% average yearly return, which is an illustration and not a forecast.</p><ul><li><strong>Baby born in 2026:</strong> the $1,000 pilot deposit plus $100 a month ($1,200 a year) for about 18 years means $22,600 of money in, growing to roughly <strong>$44,179</strong> by the end of the growth period.</li><li><strong>Child born in 2018 with the Dell $250:</strong> the $250 plus $50 a month ($600 a year) for nine years means $5,650 in, growing to roughly <strong>$7,646</strong>. The $250 alone would grow to about $460.</li></ul><p>Try your own numbers in the <a href="{tool:trump-account-calculator}">Trump account calculator</a> or the <a href="{tool:compound-interest-calculator}">compound interest calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Should I open a Trump account for my child?</h2><p>Because the account already exists for most eligible children, the real question is whether to claim it and whether to add money. Claiming costs nothing; Treasury says there is no cost to open an account. Here is a neutral checklist:</p>'
            . '<h3>Reasons to claim and contribute</h3><ul><li>Your child was born 2025 to 2028 and can receive the $1,000 federal deposit.</li><li>Your employer offers tax-free Trump account contributions.</li><li>Grandparents or relatives want a simple place to give long-term money.</li><li>You want low-cost index investing; eligible funds must charge 0.1% a year or less.</li></ul>'
            . '<h3>Reasons to hold back on extra contributions</h3><ul><li>You lack an emergency fund or carry high-interest debt.</li><li>You are saving mainly for college; a 529 plan has tax-free education withdrawals. Compare them in <a href="/trump-account-vs-529/">Trump account vs 529</a>.</li><li>You may need the money before the child turns 18; it is generally locked until then.</li><li>You want bonds or other diversification; the account holds only U.S. stock index funds during the growth period.</li></ul><p>Family contributions are not tax deductible, but they create basis, so that part comes back tax-free when withdrawn. Earnings are taxed under IRA rules after the growth period. To plan a target amount, use the <a href="{tool:savings-goal-calculator}">savings goal calculator</a>, and see the fund type in our <a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">index fund investing guide</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Avoiding Trump account scams</h2><p>Treasury says it will never ask for passwords or verification codes by email, text or phone. Its May 2026 release said initial activation emails come only from no-reply@TrumpAccounts.Treasury.gov, with later messages through the app or from addresses ending in @trumpaccount.com. No one can charge a fee to “unlock” the $1,000. Use only the official app, trumpaccounts.gov and IRS.gov.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained: who qualifies, limits and how to open one</a></li><li><a href="/trump-account-vs-529/">Trump account vs 529: which is better for your child?</a></li><li><a href="/trump-account-250-dell-employer/">Trump account $250: Dell gift and employer contributions</a></li><li><a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">Index fund investing for beginners</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4547" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Instructions for Form 4547</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/faqs/trump-accounts-frequently-asked-questions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Trump Accounts FAQ</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/treasury-irs-issue-proposed-regulations-on-how-to-open-initial-trump-accounts-under-the-one-big-beautiful-bill" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IR-2026-33</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/taxpayers-can-now-view-and-submit-trump-account-elections-in-their-irs-individual-account" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IR-2026-68</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/irb/2026-44_irb" rel="noopener" target="_blank">T.D. 10056 (IRB 2026-44)</a>; Treasury releases of <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0508" rel="noopener" target="_blank">May 28, 2026</a>, <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0554" rel="noopener" target="_blank">July 4, 2026</a> and <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0642" rel="noopener" target="_blank">October 1, 2026</a>; <a href="https://trumpaccounts.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">trumpaccounts.gov</a>.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'How do I open a Trump account for my child?', 'Most eligible children were automatically enrolled by Treasury on October 1, 2026. A parent or guardian claims and activates the account in the official Trump Accounts app or website by verifying identity and relationship. If the child was not auto-enrolled, file IRS Form 4547 online, with a tax return or on paper.' ],
            [ 'How do I sign up for the $1,000 Trump account deposit?', 'File Form 4547 and check the pilot program box on line 7. The child must be a U.S. citizen born from January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028 with a valid Social Security number. Automatic enrollment does not include this election, and the account must be claimed to receive the deposit, according to the IRS and Treasury.' ],
            [ 'Should I open a Trump account for my child?', 'Claiming the account is free, and it is required to receive the $1,000 deposit and family or employer contributions. Whether to add your own money depends on your goals: a 529 plan is usually better for college, and an emergency fund and paying off high-interest debt usually come first. The account is locked until the child turns 18.' ],
            [ 'How do I open a Trump account for kids born before 2025?', 'The steps are the same, but there is no $1,000 federal deposit. Children under 18 with a Social Security number were auto-enrolled on October 1, 2026, so claim the account in the Trump Accounts app. If no account appears, file Form 4547 before December 31 of the year the child turns 17.' ],
            [ 'Where do I file Form 4547?', 'You can submit it through the Trump Accounts app or website, through your IRS Individual Online Account, with an e-filed tax return, or on paper to the address at IRS.gov/PaperReturns. Do not attach it to Form 1040-X. If you receive Notice CP318, follow that notice to resubmit the election.' ],
            [ 'Who can open a Trump account for a child?', 'Under the Form 4547 instructions, a legal guardian, parent, adult sibling or grandparent, in that order of priority, can elect to open an account. To elect the $1,000 pilot deposit, the person must expect the child to be their qualifying child for that tax year. The person who activates the account becomes the responsible party.' ],
            [ 'When could contributions to a Trump account start?', 'Contributions have been allowed since July 4, 2026, which is also when Treasury began making the $1,000 pilot deposits. Family, friends and employers can contribute up to $5,000 a year in total for 2026 and 2027, but only after a parent or guardian has claimed the account.' ],
            [ 'Is there a deadline to open a Trump account?', 'Under the proposed regulations announced in IR-2026-33, an election must be made by December 31 of the calendar year in which the child turns 17. The IRS has not set a separate deadline for claiming an auto-enrolled account. Rules are not final, so check IRS.gov.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'trump-account-250-dell-employer',
        'title'      => 'Trump Account $250: Dell Gift and Employer Contributions',
        'keyword'    => 'trump account $250',
        'desc'       => 'Trump account $250 explained: who got the Dell gift, the $117,300 ZIP code cutoff, employer contributions up to $2,500, the $5,000 limit and tax rules.',
        'excerpt'    => 'Who received Michael and Susan Dell’s $250 Trump account deposit, how employer contributions work, and what the limits and tax rules are.',
        'answer'     => 'The Trump account $250 is a gift from Michael and Susan Dell to 25 million children born from 2016 through 2024. It went to children in ZIP codes with a median family income of $117,300 or less, and all deposits were made by October 9, 2026. Employers can also add up to $2,500 a year tax-free.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'The Dell $250 went to children born 2016 to 2024 in ZIP codes with median family income of $117,300 or less.',
            'Employers can contribute up to $2,500 a year per employee, excluded from the employee’s income.',
            'Family and employer money combined is capped at $5,000 a year for 2026 and 2027.',
            'Family contributions to a Trump account are not tax deductible.',
        ],
        'publish_at' => '2026-10-30 09:00',
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">The <strong>Trump account $250</strong> is a philanthropic deposit from Michael and Susan Dell, separate from the $1,000 federal deposit for babies. According to Invest America, the nonprofit administering the gift, the Dells invested $250 in the Trump accounts of 25 million children born between January 1, 2016 and December 31, 2024, chosen by ZIP code. This guide explains who received it, how employer contributions work, the yearly contribution limit and whether contributions are tax deductible. For program basics, see our <a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained</a> hub.</p>'
            . '<p><em>The IRS has issued temporary and proposed regulations, not final ones. Check IRS.gov and trumpaccounts.gov before you act.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: where Trump accounts stand in October 2026</h2><p>The newest verified developments on deposits, employer programs and rules:</p><ul><li><strong>July 4, 2026:</strong> contributions opened and Treasury began depositing the $1,000 pilot contributions, according to Treasury’s May 28 and July 4, 2026 releases. Treasury has not published a count of deposits made so far in the releases we reviewed.</li><li><strong>October 1, 2026:</strong> Treasury completed automatic enrollment, saying over 60 million more eligible children now have an account ready to be claimed. A parent must still claim the account, and the $1,000 still requires a Form 4547 election.</li><li><strong>Regulations:</strong> proposed rules came on March 6, 2026 (IR-2026-33), and temporary regulations (T.D. 10056, effective September 30, 2026) cover auto-enrolled accounts. Final regulations have not been issued.</li><li><strong>Portal:</strong> the Trump Accounts app launched May 28, 2026, and Form 4547 can also be filed in the IRS Individual Online Account (IR-2026-68).</li><li><strong>Dell gift:</strong> Invest America says all $250 Dell deposits were made as of October 9, 2026.</li><li><strong>Employers:</strong> Treasury issued employer guidance on August 11, 2026 and said more than 50 companies had committed to contributions.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What is the Trump account $250 from Dell?</h2><p>On December 2, 2025, the White House announced a $6.25 billion charitable commitment from Michael and Susan Dell. The original terms were $250 for “the first 25 million American children age 10 and under living in ZIP codes with median incomes below $150,000.” The aim was to reach children born too early for the $1,000 federal pilot deposit, which only covers children born 2025 through 2028.</p><p>The terms changed when Treasury moved to automatic enrollment. Invest America explains that once every eligible child had an account, far more children qualified, while the commitment stayed at $250 for 25 million children. So the money was allocated starting with the lowest-income ZIP codes until 25 million children were reached.</p>'
            . '<h2>Dell trump account: who got the $250?</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Original announcement (Dec. 2, 2025)</th><th>Final allocation (as of Oct. 9, 2026)</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Total commitment</td><td>$6.25 billion</td><td>$6.25 billion</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Amount per child</td><td>$250</td><td>$250</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Number of children</td><td>First 25 million</td><td>25 million</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Age or birth years</td><td>Age 10 and under</td><td>Born January 1, 2016 to December 31, 2024</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>ZIP code test</td><td>Median income below $150,000</td><td>Median family income of $117,300 or less (Census 2020 to 2024 ACS data)</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Other groups</td><td>Not specified</td><td>All eligible children on military bases; funds reserved for children in foster care, expected next year</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Deposit timing</td><td>Not specified</td><td>All deposits made as of October 9, 2026</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table><p>Two details matter. First, the test uses the ZIP code’s median family income, not your own household income, so a high-earning family in an included ZIP code received it and a lower-earning family in an excluded ZIP code did not. Second, Invest America says the measure is median family income, which is different from the household income figures that often appear in online searches. Invest America’s page has a ZIP code checker and links to the Census Reporter table.</p>'
            . '<h3>Is the Dell $250 the same as the federal $1,000?</h3><p>No. The $1,000 is a one-time federal pilot contribution from Treasury for U.S. citizen children born 2025 through 2028, and it requires a Form 4547 election. The Dell $250 is a private gift from Michael and Susan Dell aimed at children born 2016 through 2024, who are too old for the federal deposit. A child born in 2025 or later was outside the Dell birth years, so the two deposits generally did not overlap. Both sit in the same Trump account and are invested in the same eligible index fund.</p><h3>Dell foundation trump account FAQ: what if I enrolled early?</h3><p>Invest America says that if you claimed a Trump account or submitted Form 4547 before October 1, 2026, your child remains eligible under the rules that applied at that time, and you do not need to take further action. To see whether the $250 arrived, claim the account in the official Trump Accounts app.</p>'
            . '<h3>Dell trump account donation: is it taxable or counted toward the limit?</h3><p>The sources we reviewed do not say how the Dell deposit is classified. If it is treated as a qualified general contribution from a charity, Notice 2025-68 says it would not count toward the $5,000 yearly limit and would have no basis, so it would be taxed when eventually withdrawn. That classification is not confirmed, so check your account statements and IRS.gov.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account 530A employer match: how employer contributions work</h2><p>Trump accounts are defined in section 530A of the tax code, and employer contributions run through a companion rule, section 128. People often search for a “530A employer match,” but technically it is a section 128 Trump account contribution program. According to IRS Notice 2025-68:</p><ul><li><strong>Limit:</strong> an employer can contribute up to $2,500 a year per employee, excluded from the employee’s gross income. The cap is indexed after 2027.</li><li><strong>Per employee, not per child:</strong> an employee with two children can receive $2,500 in total across their accounts, not $2,500 each.</li><li><strong>Written plan:</strong> the employer needs a separate written plan, with rules similar to dependent care assistance programs on nondiscrimination, eligibility, notices and statements.</li><li><strong>Counts toward $5,000:</strong> section 128 contributions share the $5,000 yearly limit with family contributions.</li><li><strong>Salary reduction:</strong> an employee can contribute pre-tax through a cafeteria plan to a dependent’s Trump account, but not to their own.</li></ul><p>On August 11, 2026, Treasury announced employer guidance and said more than 50 companies had committed to contributions. Examples in that release include Vanguard, which said that from 2027 employees can direct a $1,500 employer contribution, and Visa and Franklin Templeton, which said they will match the $1,000 federal contribution for eligible children. Ask your HR department whether your employer has a program.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account contribution limit and max contribution</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Type of money</th><th>Yearly limit</th><th>Counts toward $5,000?</th><th>Creates basis?</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Parents, relatives, friends, the child</td><td>Part of $5,000</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Employer (section 128)</td><td>$2,500 per employee</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>$1,000 federal pilot deposit</td><td>One time</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Qualified general contributions (states, governments, charities)</td><td>No limit</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Qualified rollovers</td><td>No limit</td><td>No</td><td>Carries over</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table><p>The Trump account max contribution from family and employers combined is $5,000 a year for 2026 and 2027, adjusted for inflation after 2027, according to the Form 4547 instructions. The child does not need earned income. Contributions go in only during the growth period, which ends December 31 of the year before the child turns 18.</p>'
            . '<h2>Are Trump account contributions tax deductible?</h2><p>No, not for family contributions. The Form 4547 instructions state that no deduction is allowed under section 219 for any contribution to a Trump account during the growth period. A parent who puts in $5,000 gets no federal deduction. The tax benefits are elsewhere:</p><ul><li><strong>Tax-deferred growth:</strong> earnings are not taxed while the money stays in.</li><li><strong>Basis:</strong> your after-tax contributions come back tax-free when withdrawn, in proportion to the account’s total value.</li><li><strong>Employer money:</strong> up to $2,500 of employer contributions is excluded from the employee’s income, which is the main federal tax break in the program.</li></ul><p>Withdrawals of earnings and of money with no basis (the $1,000, employer contributions and general contributions) are taxed as ordinary income after the growth period, and the 10% early-withdrawal penalty can apply unless an exception such as higher education or a first home fits. Use the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> to estimate a bracket.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: $250, employer money and the $5,000 limit</h2><p>All figures assume a 7% average yearly return. They are illustrations, not forecasts.</p><ul><li><strong>The $250 alone:</strong> deposited in October 2026 for a child born in 2018, it grows to about $460 over nine years. Left invested until age 65, about 57 years, it would be roughly $11,825.</li><li><strong>Employer only:</strong> $2,500 a year from an employer for 18 years is $45,000 in, growing to about $84,998.</li><li><strong>Maximum:</strong> a baby with the $1,000 pilot deposit plus the full $5,000 a year for 18 years is $91,000 in, growing to about $173,375.</li><li><strong>Tax value of employer money:</strong> for an employee in the 22% federal bracket, $2,500 excluded from income avoids about $550 of federal income tax that year, compared with receiving the same $2,500 as salary.</li></ul><p>Check your own numbers with the <a href="{tool:trump-account-calculator}">Trump account calculator</a> or the <a href="{tool:compound-interest-calculator}">compound interest calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>$250 Trump account: what to do now</h2><ol><li><strong>Claim the account.</strong> Treasury auto-enrolled eligible children on October 1, 2026, but a parent must claim it to see deposits and allow contributions. See <a href="/how-to-open-a-trump-account/">how to open a Trump account</a>.</li><li><strong>Check the Dell deposit</strong> with Invest America’s ZIP code checker or in the app.</li><li><strong>Ask your employer</strong> about a section 128 program or a cafeteria plan option.</li><li><strong>Track the $5,000 limit</strong> across all family and employer sources so you do not over-contribute.</li><li><strong>Decide where college money goes.</strong> Compare a <a href="/trump-account-vs-529/">Trump account vs 529</a>, and read about the index funds these accounts hold in our <a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">index fund investing guide</a>.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/trump-accounts-explained/">Trump accounts explained: who qualifies, limits and how to open one</a></li><li><a href="/how-to-open-a-trump-account/">How to open a Trump account: Form 4547 step by step</a></li><li><a href="/trump-account-vs-529/">Trump account vs 529: which is better for your child?</a></li><li><a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">Index fund investing for beginners</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p><a href="https://www.investamerica.org/dell" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Invest America, Dell $250 eligibility page</a> (as of October 9, 2026); <a href="https://www.dell.org/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Michael &amp; Susan Dell Foundation</a>; <a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/releases/2025/12/landmark-dell-gift-supercharges-trump-accounts-for-americas-kids" rel="noopener" target="_blank">White House release, December 2, 2025</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-25-68.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Notice 2025-68</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4547" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Instructions for Form 4547</a>; Treasury releases of <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0602" rel="noopener" target="_blank">August 11, 2026</a> and <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0642" rel="noopener" target="_blank">October 1, 2026</a>.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
